    id: "p3-06", d: 3, title: "The Corps of Discovery",
    text: "In 1803, the United States bought a vast stretch of land from France in a deal known as the Louisiana Purchase. Few Americans knew what lay inside it. President Thomas Jefferson chose Meriwether Lewis and William Clark to lead a group, called the Corps of Discovery, across the West to the Pacific Ocean.\n\nThe expedition set out from near St. Louis in May 1804. Along the way, its members mapped rivers, described plants and animals unknown to scientists in the East, and met with dozens of Native American nations. A Shoshone woman named Sacagawea traveled with them as an interpreter. Her presence also signaled to the people they met that the group was not a war party. In Shoshone lands, the explorers traded for horses they badly needed to cross the Rocky Mountains.\n\nThe Corps reached the Pacific coast in November 1805 and returned to St. Louis in September 1806. Remarkably, only one member died during the journey of more than two years.",
